1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,456
<font color="#FFFFFF">Graph neural networks need many labeled nodes, and
labeling is expensive.</font>

2
00:00:04,536 --> 00:00:08,491
<font color="#FFFFFF">This ICLR 2022 paper, Information Gain Propagation,
rethinks graph active learning.</font>

3
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:12,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of asking an oracle for a node's exact class,</font>

4
00:00:12,607 --> 00:00:16,563
<font color="#FFFFFF">it asks a cheaper binary question: does this node
belong to a given class?</font>

5
00:00:16,643 --> 00:00:20,599
<font color="#FFFFFF">That soft label,</font>

6
00:00:20,679 --> 00:00:24,634
<font color="#FFFFFF">plus a criterion picking nodes whose information
gain propagates farthest,</font>

7
00:00:24,714 --> 00:00:28,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">gives higher accuracy at lower cost.</font>

8
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:32,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">Graph neural networks rely on large amounts of
labeled data,</font>

9
00:00:32,841 --> 00:00:34,404
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is costly to obtain.</font>

10
00:00:34,484 --> 00:00:39,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">Active learning cuts this cost by selecting the
most valuable nodes to label.</font>

11
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:44,199
<font color="#FFFFFF">But every prior method assumes an oracle can always
name a node's exact class.</font>

12
00:00:44,279 --> 00:00:47,548
<font color="#FFFFFF">When categories are many or the domain is unfamiliar,</font>

13
00:00:47,628 --> 00:00:52,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">that multi-class question is too demanding, and the
budget is spent inefficiently.</font>

14
00:00:53,110 --> 00:00:56,743
<font color="#000000">Confirming a guess is far easier than naming the
exact class.</font>

15
00:00:56,823 --> 00:01:00,456
<font color="#000000">If an expert only answers a binary yes-or-no question,</font>

16
00:01:00,536 --> 00:01:04,169
<font color="#000000">the labeling cost per node drops sharply,
especially with many classes.</font>

17
00:01:04,249 --> 00:01:07,881
<font color="#000000">Meanwhile,</font>

18
00:01:07,961 --> 00:01:11,594
<font color="#000000">existing selection criteria were built for hard
labels and only measure a single node's uncertainty.</font>

19
00:01:11,674 --> 00:01:15,307
<font color="#000000">They ignore that in a graph network,</font>

20
00:01:15,387 --> 00:01:19,020
<font color="#000000">labeling one node propagates supervision across its
k-hop neighborhood.</font>

21
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:22,446
<font color="#000000">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

22
00:01:22,526 --> 00:01:25,573
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

23
00:01:25,653 --> 00:01:28,699
<font color="#000000">a new active-learning paradigm for graph networks
that uses relaxed queries and soft labels</font>

24
00:01:28,779 --> 00:01:31,825
<font color="#000000">instead of exact-class annotation.</font>

25
00:01:31,905 --> 00:01:34,951
<font color="#000000">Second,</font>

26
00:01:35,031 --> 00:01:38,078
<font color="#000000">a node-selection criterion that maximizes how
information gain propagates through the graph.</font>

27
00:01:38,157 --> 00:01:41,204
<font color="#000000">Third,</font>

28
00:01:41,284 --> 00:01:44,330
<font color="#000000">experiments showing it beats state-of-the-art
baselines and generalizes across many graph neural network backbones.</font>

29
00:01:44,710 --> 00:01:48,218
<font color="#000000">IGP runs in a loop.</font>

30
00:01:48,298 --> 00:01:51,806
<font color="#000000">A graph network is first trained on the labeled
nodes with their soft labels.</font>

31
00:01:51,886 --> 00:01:55,394
<font color="#000000">For each candidate, the oracle is asked only
whether the predicted label is correct,</font>

32
00:01:55,474 --> 00:01:58,982
<font color="#000000">and that answer becomes a normalized soft label.</font>

33
00:01:59,062 --> 00:02:02,570
<font color="#000000">To choose nodes,</font>

34
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:06,158
<font color="#000000">IGP measures how strongly each node influences its
neighbors through propagation,</font>

35
00:02:06,238 --> 00:02:09,746
<font color="#000000">the influence magnitude, and combines it with the
information gain,</font>

36
00:02:09,826 --> 00:02:13,334
<font color="#000000">the entropy reduction, of every influenced node.</font>

37
00:02:13,414 --> 00:02:16,922
<font color="#000000">It then selects the budget-limited subset that
maximizes total propagated information gain,</font>

38
00:02:17,002 --> 00:02:20,510
<font color="#000000">updates the model, and repeats.</font>

39
00:02:20,890 --> 00:02:26,223
<font color="#FFFFFF">IGP is evaluated on five graph benchmarks: the
citation networks Cora, Citeseer,</font>

40
00:02:26,303 --> 00:02:29,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">and PubMed, the large social network Reddit,</font>

41
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:32,448
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the Open Graph Benchmark dataset ogbn-arxiv.</font>

42
00:02:32,528 --> 00:02:37,861
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, the budget is defined as true annotation
cost, not a count of labels,</font>

43
00:02:37,941 --> 00:02:41,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">since an exact query is far pricier than a binary one.</font>

44
00:02:41,595 --> 00:02:44,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">Budgets vary from two to twenty labels per class.</font>

45
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:50,689
<font color="#000000">Across every dataset and budget, IGP delivers the
highest test accuracy.</font>

46
00:02:50,769 --> 00:02:56,402
<font color="#000000">Under twenty labels per class with a GCN, it
reaches 86.4 percent on Cora,</font>

47
00:02:56,482 --> 00:03:02,579
<font color="#000000">75.8 on Citeseer, and 83.6 on PubMed, beating the
strongest prior method, GRAIN,</font>

48
00:03:02,659 --> 00:03:05,899
<font color="#000000">by 1.6 to 2.2 percent on citation networks,</font>

49
00:03:05,979 --> 00:03:10,454
<font color="#000000">with smaller but consistent gains on Reddit and
ogbn-arxiv.</font>

50
00:03:10,534 --> 00:03:16,090
<font color="#000000">As budget grows, IGP climbs fastest, and the lead
holds across backbones.</font>

51
00:03:16,470 --> 00:03:19,521
<font color="#000000">An ablation disables one component at a time.</font>

52
00:03:19,601 --> 00:03:24,114
<font color="#000000">Removing informative selection, the
propagation-aware node choice,</font>

53
00:03:24,194 --> 00:03:27,801
<font color="#000000">causes the largest drop, up to 3.2 percent on PubMed,</font>

54
00:03:27,881 --> 00:03:30,585
<font color="#000000">making it the most important ingredient.</font>

55
00:03:30,665 --> 00:03:35,525
<font color="#000000">Removing informative training with soft labels
costs up to 2.9 percent,</font>

56
00:03:35,605 --> 00:03:39,978
<font color="#000000">dropping the influence-magnitude information
quantity up to 2.1,</font>

57
00:03:40,058 --> 00:03:43,040
<font color="#000000">and removing the normalized label up to 2.4.</font>

58
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:44,710
<font color="#000000">Every piece contributes.</font>

59
00:03:45,090 --> 00:03:48,487
<font color="#000000">The headline numbers: 86.4 percent accuracy on Cora,</font>

60
00:03:48,567 --> 00:03:51,295
<font color="#000000">a 2.2 percent gain over the best baseline,</font>

61
00:03:51,375 --> 00:03:55,908
<font color="#000000">state-of-the-art results across five datasets and
four GNN backbones,</font>

62
00:03:55,988 --> 00:04:02,460
<font color="#000000">all achieved by replacing the expensive exact-class
question with one cheap binary query per node.</font>

63
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:04,279
<font color="#000000">The takeaway is simple.</font>

64
00:04:04,359 --> 00:04:06,790
<font color="#000000">Ask a cheap binary yes-or-no question,</font>

65
00:04:06,870 --> 00:04:11,877
<font color="#000000">and select nodes whose information gain propagates
farthest across the graph.</font>

66
00:04:11,957 --> 00:04:16,897
<font color="#000000">IGP makes graph active learning both more accurate
and far cheaper to label,</font>

67
00:04:16,977 --> 00:04:19,540
<font color="#000000">and works with any graph neural network.</font>
